from . import n
n("biomolecules_overview","The four classes of biomolecules","5",(1e-10,1e-6),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Life is built from carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids, all made largely of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorus and sulfur.",
 "Each class is built from small repeating units: sugars, fatty acids, amino acids and nucleotides. Their shapes and charges decide what they do.",
 "CHONPS ≈ 98 % of body atoms; ~20 amino acids, 4 DNA bases; glucose C₆H₁₂O₆ (180 g/mol); average amino acid ≈110 Da.",
 "Some organisms use additional elements (Fe, Zn, Mg, Mo, Se).",
 em="organic_functional_groups intermolecular_forces",ev="Elemental analysis of organisms",refs="BIO 3; COB 2; ORG 25; ORG 26; ORG 27",ex="protein_structure dna_structure membrane_structure carbohydrates_energy",sim="molecule")
n("carbohydrates_energy","Carbohydrates and lipids","5",(1e-10,1e-7),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Sugars store quick energy and build structures like cellulose; fats store much more energy per gram and form cell membranes.",
 "Glucose chains make starch (plant storage), glycogen (animal storage) and cellulose (plant walls). Fatty acids have long hydrocarbon tails that avoid water.",
 "Fat ≈37 kJ/g vs carbohydrate ≈17 kJ/g; cellulose β-1,4 linkage; palmitic acid C₁₆H₃₂O₂.",
 "Digestibility depends on enzymes present (e.g. cellulase absent in humans).",
 em="biomolecules_overview",ev="Calorimetry of foods",refs="BIO 3; ORG 25; ORG 27",sim="molecule")
n("protein_structure","Proteins: structure and folding","5",(1e-9,1e-7),"C2","Biochemistry",
 "A protein is a chain of amino acids that folds into a specific 3D shape set by its sequence. The shape allows it to act as an enzyme, motor, channel or building block.",
 "Four levels: sequence, local helices and sheets, overall fold and multi-chain assemblies. Folding is driven by hiding oily groups and forming hydrogen bonds. Many proteins also have flexible disordered regions.",
 "Peptide bond 0.133 nm; α-helix 3.6 residues/turn, 0.54 nm pitch; Levinthal paradox: ~10³⁰⁰ conformations but folds in ms–s; ΔG_fold≈−20 to −60 kJ/mol.",
 "Prediction of static folds is now accurate; dynamics and design remain challenging.",
 em="biomolecules_overview intermolecular_forces",ev="X-ray crystallography|Cryo-EM|NMR",refs="BIO 3; ORG 26",fref="alphafold_limits",ex="enzymes",sim="protein molecule",fr="Full dynamics of folding and multi-conformation states~open")
n("enzymes","Enzymes and catalysis in cells","5",(1e-9,1e-7),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Enzymes are protein catalysts that speed up reactions by millions of times by holding molecules in the right position. Each acts on specific substrates.",
 "Michaelis-Menten kinetics describes how rate saturates with substrate. Inhibitors and allosteric regulators tune enzymes.",
 "v=V_max[S]/(K_m+[S]); k_cat/K_m up to ~10⁸–10⁹ M⁻¹s⁻¹ (diffusion limit); catalase k_cat≈10⁷ s⁻¹.",
 "Idealised for single substrate steady state.",
 em="protein_structure reaction_kinetics",ev="Enzyme assays|Crystal structures with substrate analogues",refs="BIO 6; MIC 7",ex="metabolism_overview",sim="protein")
n("atp_energy","ATP: the cell's energy currency","5",(1e-9,1e-6),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "ATP stores energy in its phosphate bonds. Cells break it to ADP to power movement, building and pumping, then rebuild it from food or light.",
 "A human recycles roughly their own body weight in ATP every day. Many reactions are coupled to ATP hydrolysis.",
 "ATP+H₂O→ADP+Pi, ΔG°′≈−30.5 kJ/mol (≈−50 kJ/mol in cells); ~10²¹ ATP molecules/s used by a human at rest.",
 "Used by nearly all life; other nucleotides (GTP) also carry energy.",
 em="chemistry_gibbs biomolecules_overview",ev="Isotope labelling|Calorimetry",refs="BIO 6; ANP 24",ex="metabolism_overview",sim="molecule mitochondrion")
n("metabolism_overview","Metabolism: glycolysis, Krebs cycle and respiration","5",(1e-9,1e-6),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Cells extract energy from glucose in stages: glycolysis, the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain. Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in humans.",
 "Glycolysis yields 2 ATP in the cytoplasm; the rest of up to ~30 ATP per glucose comes from the mitochondrion.",
 "C₆H₁₂O₆+6O₂→6CO₂+6H₂O, ΔG°′=−2870 kJ/mol; ~30–32 ATP per glucose; fermentation 2 ATP.",
 "Yield varies with proton leak and tissue.",
 em="enzymes atp_energy",ev="Metabolic maps|Radiolabel tracing",refs="BIO 7; COB 4",ex="oxidative_phosphorylation",sim="mitochondrion")
n("oxidative_phosphorylation","Chemiosmosis and ATP synthase","5",(1e-9,1e-7),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Electrons from food pass along protein complexes in the mitochondrial membrane, pumping protons across it. The proton flow back through ATP synthase, a tiny rotary motor, makes ATP.",
 "Peter Mitchell's chemiosmotic idea unites respiration and photosynthesis. The enzyme rotates about 100 times per second.",
 "Proton motive force Δp=Δψ−(2.3RT/F)ΔpH ≈ 150–200 mV; ~3–4 H⁺ per ATP; F₁F₀ rotates ~100–150 Hz.",
 "Common to mitochondria, chloroplasts and bacteria.",
 em="electrochemistry metabolism_overview",ev="Mitchell 1961 (Nobel 1978)|Single-molecule rotation imaging",refs="BIO 7",ex="mitochondria_org",sim="mitochondrion membrane")
n("photosynthesis_light","Photosynthesis","5",(1e-9,1e-3),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Plants, algae and some bacteria capture sunlight to turn CO₂ and water into sugar and oxygen. This process supplies food and the oxygen in air.",
 "The light reactions use chlorophyll to split water and make ATP and NADPH; the Calvin cycle uses them to fix carbon with the enzyme Rubisco.",
 "6CO₂+6H₂O+photons→C₆H₁₂O₆+6O₂; ΔG°′≈+2870 kJ/mol; theoretical max conversion ≈4.6 % for C₃ plants; photons 400–700 nm.",
 "Rubisco also reacts with O₂ (photorespiration), lowering efficiency.",
 em="electrochemistry oxidative_phosphorylation atomic_spectra",ev="Isotope tracer experiments (¹⁴C)|Action spectra",refs="BIO 8; COB 5",ex="chloroplast_org biosphere_energy",sim="chloroplast leaf")
n("dna_structure","DNA structure and replication","5",(1e-9,1e-6),"C1","Molecular biology",
 "DNA is a double helix of two strands, with the bases A-T and G-C pairing across the middle. Its sequence stores genetic information and it is copied before cells divide.",
 "Each strand serves as a template for a new partner (semi-conservative replication), carried out by DNA polymerase with proofreading, giving about one error per 10⁹–10¹⁰ bases.",
 "Helix diameter 2.0 nm, 0.34 nm per base pair, 10.5 bp per turn; human genome ≈3.1×10⁹ bp ≈ 2 m long per cell; error rate ≈10⁻⁹–10⁻¹⁰.",
 "Universal in cellular life; some viruses use RNA genomes.",
 em="intermolecular_forces biomolecules_overview",ev="Franklin X-ray diffraction|Meselson-Stahl|Sequencing",refs="BIO 14; COB 9; ORG 28",ex="central_dogma",sim="dna molecule")
n("central_dogma","Central dogma: transcription and translation","5",(1e-9,1e-7),"C1","Molecular biology",
 "DNA is copied into RNA, and RNA is read by ribosomes to build proteins. A three-letter genetic code in RNA specifies each amino acid.",
 "The code has 64 codons for 20 amino acids and stop signals, nearly universal. Exceptions exist, and RNA viruses reverse the direction using reverse transcriptase.",
 "64 codons→20 aa+3 stops; AUG start; ribosome adds ≈15–20 aa/s (bacteria); RNA polymerase ≈50 nt/s.",
 "Near-universal code with documented variants.",
 em="dna_structure",ev="Nirenberg-Khorana code|Ribosome structures",refs="BIO 15; BIO 16; COB 9; MIC 11",ex="gene_regulation protein_structure",sim="rna ribosome protein")
n("gene_regulation","Gene regulation and epigenetics","5",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Molecular biology",
 "Cells with the same DNA become different types by switching genes on and off. Proteins bind DNA near genes, and chemical tags on DNA and histones adjust accessibility.",
 "Bacteria regulate genes by operons like lac; animals use enhancers and chromatin states. Epigenetic marks can be inherited through cell division.",
 "Lac operon induction by allolactose; DNA methylation at CpG; ~1,600 human transcription factors; enhancer-promoter loops 10⁴–10⁶ bp.",
 "Transgenerational epigenetic inheritance in humans is not established.",
 em="central_dogma",ev="Jacob-Monod|ChIP-seq maps",refs="BIO 16; MIC 11",ex="development_biology cell_types",fr="Human transgenerational epigenetic effects debated~open",sim="chromatin nucleosome")
n("chromatin_genome","Chromatin, chromosomes and the genome","5",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Molecular biology",
 "DNA is wrapped around protein spools (nucleosomes) and folded into chromosomes. Humans have 46 chromosomes holding about 20,000 protein-coding genes.",
 "Only about 1–2 % of the genome codes for protein; the rest includes regulatory regions, repeats and RNA genes. A gap-free human sequence was completed in 2022.",
 "Nucleosome: 147 bp around 8 histones; 3.1 Gbp; ≈20 000 protein-coding genes; mitochondrial genome 16.6 kb.",
 "Function of much non-coding DNA is still being mapped.",
 em="dna_structure",ev="Human Genome Project 2003|Telomere-to-telomere assembly",refs="BIO 14; BIO 17",ex="genetics_inheritance",sim="nucleosome chromatin nucleus_cell")
n("crispr_biotech","CRISPR and genetic engineering","7",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Biotechnology",
 "CRISPR is a bacterial immune system adapted as a tool to edit DNA at chosen sites. It is used in research, agriculture and the first approved gene-editing therapy.",
 "A guide RNA directs the Cas9 enzyme to a matching DNA sequence and cuts it, after which cells repair the cut. Careful design limits off-target edits.",
 "20-nt guide RNA; PAM NGG for SpCas9; edit efficiencies vary 10–90 %.",
 "Off-target effects and delivery limit therapies.",
 em="central_dogma",ev="Doudna and Charpentier 2012|Clinical use for sickle-cell disease",refs="BIO 17; MIC 12",fr="Safe delivery and long-term effects~open")
n("membrane_structure","Cell membranes and lipid bilayers","6",(1e-9,1e-6),"C1","Cell biology",
 "A cell is wrapped in a two-layer sheet of lipids whose water-loving heads face outside and oily tails face inward. This barrier keeps the cell's contents separate.",
 "Proteins embedded in the bilayer act as channels, pumps and receptors. The membrane is fluid, with lipids and proteins drifting sideways.",
 "Bilayer thickness ≈4–5 nm; lateral D≈10⁻¹² m²/s for lipids; membrane potential ≈−70 mV in neurons.",
 "Fluid mosaic model; lipid rafts remain debated.",
 em="intermolecular_forces soft_matter carbohydrates_energy",ev="Electron microscopy|Fluorescence recovery",refs="BIO 5; COB 3; ANP 3",ex="membrane_transport",sim="membrane")
n("membrane_transport","Membrane transport and osmosis","6",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Small gases and water cross membranes freely, while ions and large molecules need channels or pumps. Water moves toward higher solute concentration by osmosis.",
 "Pumps like the sodium-potassium ATPase spend ATP to build gradients, which cells then use for signals, nutrient uptake and ATP production.",
 "Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP; osmotic Π=iMRT; aquaporin ≈3×10⁹ water/s per channel.",
 "Passive diffusion follows concentration gradients; active transport requires energy.",
 em="membrane_structure heat_diffusion chemistry_solutions",ev="Patch clamp|Red blood cell osmosis",refs="BIO 5; ANP 3",ex="action_potential",sim="membrane cell:animal")
n("cell_biology_intro","Cells: the unit of life","6",(1e-6,1e-4),"C1","Cell biology",
 "All living things are made of cells, cells come from other cells, and a cell is the smallest unit that can live on its own. Cells range from bacteria to neurons.",
 "Prokaryotic cells lack a nucleus; eukaryotic cells contain organelles. Cells keep their internal chemistry different from outside by controlling what crosses the membrane.",
 "Bacterium 1–5 μm, animal cell 10–30 μm, human egg ≈100 μm; ≈3.7×10¹³ human cells.",
 "Viruses are not cells.",
 em="biomolecules_overview membrane_structure",ev="Microscopy since Hooke and Leeuwenhoek",refs="BIO 4; COB 3; ANP 3",ex="organelles_overview cell_cycle",sim="cell:animal cell:plant cell:bacterium")
n("organelles_overview","Organelles: nucleus, ER, Golgi, lysosomes","6",(1e-7,1e-5),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Eukaryotic cells divide work among compartments: the nucleus stores DNA, the ER and Golgi build and ship proteins, and lysosomes recycle waste.",
 "Proteins are labelled with signal sequences that route them to the right place. Vesicles carry cargo between compartments.",
 "Nucleus ≈6 μm; nuclear pore ≈120 MDa complex; ribosomes ≈20–25 nm; lysosome pH≈4.5–5.",
 "Details differ among kingdoms.",
 em="cell_biology_intro central_dogma",ev="Electron microscopy|Pulse-chase experiments",refs="BIO 4; COB 3",sim="nucleus_cell er golgi lysosome ribosome")
n("mitochondria_org","Mitochondria and endosymbiosis","6",(1e-7,1e-5),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Mitochondria are the cell's power plants and descend from bacteria that took up residence in an ancestor of all complex cells about 2 billion years ago. Chloroplasts arose similarly from cyanobacteria.",
 "They have their own small DNA and ribosomes. The origin of the host cell is debated, with archaea from the Asgard group as leading candidates.",
 "Mitochondrion ≈1–2 μm; mtDNA 16.6 kb (37 genes); ≈10³ per cell; cristae increase surface area for ATP synthase.",
 "Endosymbiosis itself is settled; host identity debated.",
 em="oxidative_phosphorylation cell_biology_intro",ev="Mitochondrial DNA similarity to α-proteobacteria|Double membrane",refs="BIO 4; BIO 7; COB 3",fr="Details of eukaryogenesis from archaea plus bacterium~open",sim="mitochondrion")
n("chloroplast_org","Chloroplasts and plant cells","6",(1e-7,1e-4),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Chloroplasts are green organelles where photosynthesis happens. Plant cells also have rigid cellulose walls and a large water-filled vacuole.",
 "Stacks of membranes called thylakoids hold chlorophyll and the electron-transport machinery. Turgor pressure from the vacuole keeps soft plants upright.",
 "Chloroplast 4–10 μm; ≈50–100 per leaf cell; chlorophyll absorbs ≈430 and 662 nm; turgor 0.3–1 MPa.",
 "Plants only; algae vary.",
 em="photosynthesis_light mitochondria_org",ev="Electron microscopy|Isolated chloroplast Hill reaction",refs="BIO 4; BIO 8; COB 3",sim="chloroplast cell:plant leaf")
n("cell_cycle","Cell division: mitosis and meiosis","6",(1e-6,1e-4),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Cells copy their DNA and split into two identical daughters by mitosis. Meiosis halves the chromosome number to make eggs and sperm and shuffles genes.",
 "Checkpoint proteins stop division if DNA is damaged. Loss of these controls is a hallmark of cancer.",
 "Human cell cycle ≈24 h; M phase ≈1 h; meiosis I recombination ~1–2 crossovers per chromosome arm.",
 "Bacteria divide by binary fission.",
 em="dna_structure cell_biology_intro",ev="Chromosome staining|Cyclin-CDK discovery",refs="BIO 10; BIO 11; COB 6",ex="cancer_biology genetics_inheritance",sim="chromatin cell:animal")
n("cell_signaling","Cell signalling and apoptosis","6",(1e-9,1e-3),"C1","Cell biology",
 "Cells communicate using chemical messengers that bind receptors, triggering relay chains inside the cell. Cells can also self-destruct (apoptosis) when damaged or no longer needed.",
 "Hormones, growth factors and neurotransmitters use different receptor types. Apoptosis shapes fingers in development and removes damaged cells.",
 "G-protein-coupled receptors ≈800 in humans; cAMP second messenger; caspase cascade.",
 "Cross-talk makes pathways networks not lines.",
 em="membrane_transport enzymes",ev="Receptor binding assays|Knockout studies",refs="BIO 9; ANP 17",ex="endocrine_physiology cancer_biology",sim="membrane")
n("cytoskeleton_motors","Cytoskeleton and molecular motors","6",(1e-9,1e-4),"C1","Cell biology",
 "A network of protein filaments gives cells shape and acts as tracks for motor proteins, which carry cargo and make muscles contract.",
 "Kinesin walks along microtubules in 8-nanometre steps, burning one ATP per step.",
 "Microtubule diameter 25 nm; actin 7 nm; kinesin step 8 nm, force ≈6 pN, speed ≈0.8 μm/s; myosin step ≈5–10 nm.",
 "Dynamics regulated by many accessory proteins.",
 em="protein_structure atp_energy",ev="Single-molecule optical tweezers",refs="BIO 4; COB 3",ex="muscle_contraction",sim="cell:animal protein")
n("viruses","Viruses","6",(2e-8,3e-7),"C1","Microbiology",
 "Viruses are packages of genetic material in a protein shell that reproduce only inside host cells. They are not cells and carry no metabolism of their own.",
 "They hijack the cell's machinery to make copies. Vaccines train immunity against viral proteins; antivirals target specific viral enzymes.",
 "SARS-CoV-2 ≈100 nm, 30 kb RNA; T4 phage ≈200 nm, 169 kb DNA; ΦX174 5.4 kb; mutation rates RNA ≈10⁻⁴–10⁻⁶/nt.",
 "Their classification as 'alive' is a definitional debate.",
 em="central_dogma",ev="Electron microscopy|Phage experiments",refs="BIO 21; MIC 6",ex="immune_system",sim="rna protein")
n("bacteria_archaea","Bacteria, archaea and microbes","6",(1e-7,1e-5),"C1","Microbiology",
 "Microbes are the most abundant and diverse life, living from deep rocks to our guts. Bacteria and archaea are single-celled without a nucleus, and microbes drive Earth's chemical cycles.",
 "Bacteria can swap genes sideways (conjugation, transformation). The gut microbiome affects digestion, but many claimed links to disease are still correlations.",
 "≈10³⁰ prokaryote cells on Earth; E. coli doubling ≈20 min, genome 4.6 Mb; human gut ≈10¹³–10¹⁴ microbes.",
 "Causal health effects of microbiome remain C3.",
 em="cell_biology_intro",ev="16S rRNA phylogeny|Metagenomics",refs="BIO 22; MIC 4; MIC 9",ex="biogeochemical_cycles",fr="Causal roles of the microbiome in disease~open",sim="cell:bacterium")
n("genetics_inheritance","Mendelian and molecular inheritance","6",(1e-9,1e-1),"C1","Genetics",
 "Traits pass from parents to offspring in discrete units (genes). Each parent gives one copy; dominant versions mask recessive ones.",
 "Linked genes, sex chromosomes and mutations extend Mendel's rules. Most traits are polygenic, shaped by many genes and the environment.",
 "Monohybrid cross 3:1, dihybrid 9:3:3:1; Hardy-Weinberg p²+2pq+q²=1; human mutation rate ≈1.2×10⁻⁸ per bp per generation.",
 "Polygenic traits follow quantitative-genetic models.",
 em="chromatin_genome cell_cycle",ev="Mendel's peas|Pedigree analysis|GWAS",refs="BIO 12; BIO 13; COB 8",ex="natural_selection",sim="chromatin human",fr="'Missing heritability' of complex traits~open")
n("natural_selection","Evolution by natural selection","8",(1e-9,1e7),"C1","Evolution",
 "Individuals vary, some variants leave more offspring, and traits that are heritable accumulate over generations. This process explains the adaptation and diversity of life.",
 "Other forces matter too: mutation, genetic drift, gene flow and sexual selection. Observed evolution includes antibiotic resistance and the Lenski long-term E. coli experiment.",
 "Breeder's equation R=h²S; selection coefficient s; fixation probability ≈2s for new beneficial mutation; Δp=sp(1−p).",
 "Applies wherever there is variation, heredity and differential reproduction.",
 em="genetics_inheritance",ev="Fossil record|Phylogenies|Peppered moths|Lenski experiment",refs="BIO 18; BIO 19; COB 11",ex="common_descent speciation_biodiversity",fr="Extended Evolutionary Synthesis debate~open|Predictability of evolution~open",sim="bird")
n("common_descent","Common descent and phylogenetics","8",(1e-9,1e7),"C1","Evolution",
 "All living things share a single family tree. DNA similarity, anatomy and fossils show how species are related.",
 "Molecular clocks use mutation counts to date branch points. Nearly universal genetic code and shared genes point to a common ancestor.",
 "Human-chimp genome ≈98.8 % similar (SNPs); split ≈6–7 Ma; molecular clock d=2μt.",
 "Horizontal gene transfer blurs the tree among microbes.",
 em="natural_selection",ev="Comparative genomics|Transitional fossils (Tiktaalik)",refs="BIO 20; COB 12",ex="speciation_biodiversity")
n("speciation_biodiversity","Speciation and the diversity of life","8",(1e-6,1e7),"C1","Evolution",
 "New species form when populations are isolated and diverge. About two million species are described, and the true number is far higher.",
 "Estimates of total species range from several million to much larger numbers if microbes are included. Mass extinctions have repeatedly reset diversity.",
 "~2.2 million described eukaryote species; ≈8.7 M eukaryotes estimated (2011); five mass extinctions.",
 "Total species count estimates remain uncertain.",
 em="natural_selection",ev="Island radiations (finches, cichlids)|Ring species",refs="BIO 18; BIO 47",fr="True species total uncertain~open",sim="bird bee")
n("mass_extinctions","Mass extinctions and major transitions","8",(1e6,1e7),"C1","Evolution",
 "Life's history has major transitions (eukaryotes, multicellularity, land) and five mass extinctions. The last, 66 million years ago, wiped out non-avian dinosaurs after an asteroid impact.",
 "The end-Permian extinction ~252 Ma was the worst, linked to volcanism. The Cambrian explosion ~540 Ma saw most animal body plans appear.",
 "Chicxulub impactor ≈10 km, crater ≈180 km; Permian loss ≈80–95 % marine species; K-Pg boundary iridium anomaly.",
 "Causes of some events debated.",
 em="natural_selection deep_time",ev="Iridium layer (Alvarez)|Fossil diversity curves",refs="BIO 20; AST 14",ex="human_evolution")
n("human_evolution","Human evolution","8",(1,1e7),"C2","Evolution",
 "Humans share ancestors with chimpanzees from ~6–7 million years ago. Homo sapiens arose in Africa and interbred with Neanderthals and Denisovans.",
 "Bipedalism came first, large brains later. Ancient DNA shows that non-African people carry ≈1–2 % Neanderthal ancestry.",
 "H. sapiens ≈300 ka; brain volume ≈1350 cm³; Neanderthal DNA ≈1–2 % in non-Africans.",
 "Dates revised as new fossils and DNA appear.",
 em="common_descent mass_extinctions",ev="Fossils (Lucy, Turkana boy)|Ancient DNA",refs="BIO 29",sim="human")
n("origin_of_life","Origin of life","8",(1e-9,1e-3),"C3","Origin of life",
 "How non-living chemistry became living cells is still unknown. Leading ideas include RNA-first replication, hydrothermal vents, and wet-dry pools on land.",
 "Experiments make amino acids, sugars and nucleotides from simple starting molecules, and some RNAs act as catalysts. A step from chemistry to self-replicating cells has not been reproduced.",
 "Miller-Urey 1953 produced amino acids; ribozyme polymerases extend ≈100+ nt; protocell vesicle size ≈1 μm.",
 "No consensus; any scenario must fit early-Earth geochemistry.",
 em="biomolecules_overview chemistry_gibbs stereochemistry_chirality",ev="Prebiotic chemistry|Ribozymes|Meteorite organics",refs="BIO 2; AST 30",fref="luca_age bennu_samples",fr="Start of replication and the genetic code~open|Homochirality and protocells~open",ex="rna_world")
n("rna_world","RNA world hypothesis","8",(1e-9,1e-6),"C2","Origin of life",
 "RNA can both store information and catalyse reactions, so it may have preceded DNA and proteins. The ribosome's core is made of RNA, a clue.",
 "It leaves open how RNA first formed and how the shift to DNA and proteins occurred.",
 "Hammerhead and RNase P ribozymes; peptidyl transferase is rRNA; non-enzymatic copying yields ≲1 % errors only with special conditions.",
 "Plausible stage, not a proven origin.",
 em="origin_of_life central_dogma",ev="Ribozymes|Ribosome structure (Nobel 2009)",refs="BIO 15",fref="bennu_samples")
n("luca","Last universal common ancestor (LUCA)","8",(1e-6,1e-5),"C3","Origin of life",
 "All present life descends from one ancestral population, the last universal common ancestor, which lived billions of years ago.",
 "Shared genes across bacteria and archaea hint at what LUCA needed, but its age and lifestyle are estimated indirectly.",
 "Universal genes ≈ a few hundred; earliest widely accepted fossils ≈3.5 Ga.",
 "Dates depend on molecular-clock calibration.",
 em="origin_of_life",ev="Universal gene sets|Stromatolites",refs="BIO 20",fref="luca_age")
